Skerry-Chop
Ingredients: ZOUK + FUSION / JAZZ ROCK + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Rhythm Electric Guitar + 1970s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Isolated lighthouse keepers along the sub-Arctic Svalbard archipelago broadcast hypnotic, rhythmically warped dance grooves across shortwave frequencies to keep icebound freighters from madness during five-month polar nights.

Skerry-Chop is the sound of tropical carnival music exiled to an iron tower in an ice sheet. The sun-drenched, syncopated bounce of French Antillean zouk and cadence-lypso is radically inverted: slowed to an ominous, prowling gait, stripped of festive brass, and plunged into the angular harmonic dissonance of 1970s jazz-rock fusion. At its core is a furiously tight, razor-scraped rhythm electric guitar—drier than bone, playing complex polyrhythmic chops that lock with subterranean bass grooves, funeral church organs, and maritime klaxons to form an addictive, dread-soaked nocturnal funk.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 88–104 BPM. The foundational French Caribbean ti-bwa and cadence kick-snare patterns are mutated into asymmetrical time signatures (7/8 and 11/8) that lurch with the violent, swaying heave of a storm-tossed vessel.
- Harmonic Palette: Phrygian dominant and Locrian modes colliding with 1970s jazz-fusion minor-ninth and sharp-eleventh chord voicings; brooding, unresolved tension that refuses pop resolution.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Mercury-Bath Fresnel Phasing: Rhythm guitars are recorded through directional ribbon microphones set inside the rotating lantern room, using the physical sweep of the Fresnel lens drive and open saltwater vents to generate a natural, doppler-phased acoustic flanging.
  2. Nautical Shortwave Tape Crushing: Tracks are bounced through high-frequency maritime radio transmitters and captured back on 2-inch tape, creating crisp tape hiss, harmonic crackle, and low-end proximity effect.
  3. Sub-Zero Gated Reverbs: Drums and percussion decay instantly as if played in open glacial air, juxtaposed against immense, dank stone-well echoes.
- Dynamics: Claustrophobic, locked-groove tension punctuated by sudden, explosive fusion breakdowns where time signatures splinter before slamming instantly back into the hypnotizing guitar skank.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the winter of 1976 inside the Hopen Island and Cape Linné light stations, Skerry-Chop was invented by bored Scandinavian radiomen and displaced Antillean merchant sailors stranded by winter pack ice. With nothing to do between weather logs but tend oil lamps and tinker with salvaged electric guitars, they rigged custom transmitters to beam illegal, private broadcasts to Russian colliers, Norwegian trawlers, and sealers trapped in the frozen gloom. A cult subculture of "Wick-Turners" formed: mariners began dressing in heavy oilskin coats dyed deep soot-black, adorned with intricate braided signal cords and antique nautical calipers worn as jewelry. Radio shacks became clandestine dance floors, where crews danced the bal-balisage—a slow, hip-locking shuffle designed to prevent frostbite and stave off cabin fever.

Typical Instruments: 1970s Fender Stratocaster (bridge pickup, heavily muted rhythm guitar chop), Fender Rhodes electric piano ran through vintage ring modulators, fretless bass, Moog Minimoog (drone sub-bass), altered Antillean ti-bwa woodblocks struck with iron wrenches, battered acoustic drum kits, Hammond B3 organ, shortwave radio receiver feedback.

For Fans Of: Soft Machine, Kassav' (at 33 RPM played in a tomb), Magma, Goblin, Weather Report.

